========================================================================================================================
PART 7.  MUTATION CONTROLS.
========================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 MUTATION CONTROL: with nu = 1 the same pipeline is plain Newtonian gravity, and on the measured dispersions at a stellar-population Upsilon_V = 1.6 the four clusters must sit near zero -- they contain no dark matter.  They do, which is the independent evidence that the geometry, the estimator and the photometry in this script are right and that the negative MOND residual is the boost and not a bookkeeping error   (Newtonian residuals at Ups=1.6: Pal 4 -0.109, Pal 14 +0.146, Pal 3 +0.719, NGC 2419 +0.018; median +0.082 dex)
  [PASS] M2 MUTATION CONTROL on the data: inflating every cluster dispersion by sqrt(2) must move every residual by exactly +log10(2) and nothing else may respond   (max deviation 2.78e-16 dex)
  [PASS] M3 GEOMETRY CONTROL: the (4/3) deprojection g05 applies to the projected half-light radius must land on Baumgardt's independently tabulated 3-D half-MASS radius.  It does, to 3 per cent, for all four clusters.  The radius is NOT one of the errors here   ((4/3) r_h,l against tabulated r_h,m: Pal 4 0.3%, Pal 14 2.4%, Pal 3 2.7%, NGC 2419 0.1%)
  [FAIL] M4 (THE CIRCULARITY, AND IT FAILS) Baumgardt's sigma0 is the output of a NEWTONIAN N-body model fitted to the cluster.  Substituting it for a measurement therefore imports the Newtonian answer by construction: at Upsilon_V = 1.6 the four sigma0 values scatter by only 0.10 dex about Newton, where the four MEASURED dispersions scatter by 0.35 dex.  A quantity that is Newtonian by construction cannot be used as the observed side of a test of whether gravity is Newtonian.  If Pal 3's 22-star measurement is thought unreliable -- and at a Newtonian M/L_V near 8 it may well be binary-inflated -- the honest move is to DROP Pal 3, not to replace it with a Newtonian model output; dropping it leaves the cluster median at the Pal 4 / NGC 2419 / Pal 14 value   (Newtonian residuals at Ups=1.6 from MODEL sigma0: Pal 4 -0.192, Pal 14 +0.134, Pal 3 +0.064, NGC 2419 +0.075 (scatter 0.145); from MEASURED bins: Pal 4 -0.109, Pal 14 +0.146, Pal 3 +0.719, NGC 2419 +0.018 (scatter 0.366))
     Pal 3 dropped entirely, measured dispersions elsewhere: residuals -0.864, -0.763, -0.269, median -0.763 dex
  [FAIL] M5 (BOTH-FOOTINGS VIOLATION, AND IT FAILS) g05's check S5 is computed on the canonical footing ONLY -- a0c is hard-coded in its globular-cluster block and in the dwarf comparison set it is compared against.  On the alt footing a_0 = 1.13e-10 the dwarf spheroidals over the clusters' acceleration range sit essentially ON the kernel, not above it.  The clusters stay below on both footings, so the GAP survives, but the half of the claim that says the dwarfs 'sit above it' at these accelerations holds on one footing of the two   (dwarf median over 0.01 <= x_i <= 1.2: canonical +0.114 dex on N=12, alt -0.007 dex on N=11)

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT
========================================================================================================================
  THE CLAIM'S DIRECTION SURVIVES.  ITS NUMBERS DO NOT.

  The arithmetic inside g05's globular-cluster block is correct: I reproduce all four residuals and the
  median -0.859 to 0.0005 dex with an independent implementation (V1).  The errors are in the INPUTS.

  1. Pal 3.  g05 uses 0.80 km/s, which is column 22 of Baumgardt's parameter table -- sigma0, the CENTRAL
     dispersion of his fitted N-body MODEL -- and feeds it to a Wolf et al. 2010 estimator defined at the
     half-light radius.  Baumgardt's MEASURED dispersion for Pal 3, from the same 22 stars, is 1.70
     +0.38/-0.30 km/s.  Pal 3 is the only one of the four where model and measurement disagree (they agree
     to 10 per cent for Pal 4 and Pal 14), and it is the one g05 replaces.  Pal 3 goes from -0.844 to
     -0.190.
     This repository's own committed h93 uses 1.70 and concludes the opposite about Pal 3 (its check 93f).
  2. NGC 2419.  Same substitution, smaller: the model central 5.10 km/s used at r_h,l where the measured
     profile interpolates to 4.77.  An aperture error, -0.058 dex.
  3. Pal 14.  g05 takes the lower of two dispersions for the same 16 stars, reversing h93's stated
     convention of carrying the framework-favourable one.  Worth +0.543 dex.
  4. The Upsilon_V = 1 caveat is computed as median + log10(2), the Newtonian scaling, on objects at
     x_i = 0.013-0.061 where the correct exponent is about 0.55.  Rerun, it is -0.678, not -0.558.

  NET: the quoted median -0.859 dex becomes -0.516 dex on the measured dispersions this repository
  already holds on disk -- a +0.343 dex correction.  Two of the four quoted per-cluster numbers
  move by more than half a dex.  ALL FOUR input choices ran toward the claim.

  WHAT STILL STANDS (V9, V10, M1).  Under every dispersion choice, Upsilon_V from 1 to 2, both footings and
  both external-field prescriptions, the clusters remain below the kernel (worst case -0.264 dex) and
  the dwarf spheroidals at the same x_i remain above it, with a gap of at least
  +0.289 dex.  The Newtonian mutation puts the clusters on zero at Ups = 1.6,
  which is what a dark-matter-free system should do.  So 'support type does not predict the sign of the
  residual' is NOT refuted -- but it must be quoted at a cluster median near -0.52 dex, not -0.86, and
  Pal 3 must be quoted as the object that argues the other way, not as a -0.844 point in favour.

